WEBVTT


1
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:13.810
One thing that I always enjoyed about building is like getting in the zone to just focus


2
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:18.840
on the craft, the stuff that I like to do.


3
00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:26.340
And that kind of fills me up with energy, and then I can use that energy together with


4
00:00:26.341 --> 00:00:27.020
my kids.


5
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.180
I look forward to this every year, you know, getting to see everybody in person.


6
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:47.020
It's really nice.


7
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:53.190
This year we'll be in Copenhagen, I'm based in Copenhagen, and I'm really looking forward


8
00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:54.120
to seeing everyone.


9
00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.070
There's people I never realized I hadn't met in person who I'm seeing here, where it feels


10
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.220
like you know them so well that you don't even quite register.


11
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:09.940
I like the people at this company more than any other job that I've ever had.


12
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:15.250
It's just clear from the product, from the marketing, from the packaging, from like everything


13
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.540
around the product, that people really care and people are really good at what they do.


14
00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:24.560
Just a really high pedigree of backgrounds and experiences.


15
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:29.690
A lot of people who'd founded their own companies and had like a real depth of perspective in


16
00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:30.440
their work.


17
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:35.640
I'm in Belgrade.


18
00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:40.130
If you've ever seen those socialist style, brutalist buildings, I live in one of those


19
00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:41.760
big concrete slabs.


20
00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:48.250
In Omaha, nobody knows what I do, nobody understands what I do.


21
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.800
I think there's a lot of people that don't want to be constrained by like the cost of


22
00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:57.460
living and don't want to be forced into like a particular lifestyle.


23
00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.020
You don't have to really sacrifice anything.


24
00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:08.160
It's kind of amazing right, using linear while building it.


25
00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:13.110
I find that I'm a lot more creative when it's like a tool that I use myself and I have my


26
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:17.590
own opinions and ideas and can like be an expert in my own right.


27
00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:22.590
I think the biggest thing is probably kind of getting feedback on your ideas, given that


28
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.420
it's not very high of a barrier to ship something to internal.


29
00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:30.790
If you have an idea, you'll just try it out, you'll change the product, ship it internally.


30
00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:34.320
You get to see very fast results because you're using it all day long.


31
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.300
It's like, for me, that's kind of just an addictive cycle.


32
00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:46.040
I mean, there's an efficiency of words you can say.


33
00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.900
The feedback that you get is very direct and very to the point.


34
00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:59.160
If you get feedback of, let's make this better, that actually implies that it's really good.


35
00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.850
I guess some people like working in a big organization and are really good at it, like


36
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.350
really good at understanding the hierarchy, the politics, like being able to navigate


37
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:17.920
in that space, that's kind of a skill you cannot use at Linear.


38
00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:24.130
There's like no presentations, there's no slides, there's no like formal pitch you have


39
00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:25.520
to make to convince people.


40
00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.640
We don't have these processes that would take away from focus.


41
00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:36.550
It's amazing the amount of work that you can get done if you're not being pulled into meetings


42
00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:37.380
all the time.


43
00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:40.200
My calendar is pretty empty, really.


44
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:47.320
Things are kind of completely async and kind of observation based.


45
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.960
Lots of like ad hoc riffing sessions with people.


46
00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:59.000
It's really a lot of space to express myself, basically, and put my taste into what I'm making.


47
00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.580
In software, you're building sandcastles.


48
00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:06.810
I think it's very creative.


49
00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.820
I think it's actually more about gardening or nursing something, planting a little tree,


50
00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:13.580
and then it turns out to be something else.


51
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.460
It's constantly evolving, right?


52
00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:23.720
Creative jobs are tricky emotionally because you're putting a lot of yourself into them.


53
00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.460
It's really self-critical.


54
00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:32.200
If I don't do something to balance that out, I found it to be really detrimental.


55
00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:44.580
When I think of balance, I think about having kind of power over where you're spending your time and energy.


56
00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:47.600
My mental health probably would not have been this good.


57
00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:56.860
My stress levels have managed to stay pretty low, you know, despite having a newborn and then a baby.


58
00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.820
My wife and I, we eat lunch together every day.


59
00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:03.530
When it's nice out, we sit on the porch, enjoy the sun while we eat food together.


60
00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:04.680
You're on a winning team.


61
00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:09.880
You don't have to be like crushing it every single day because, you know,


62
00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:17.340
anyone that's a parent is going to tell you that you're not going to be on top of your game every single day.


63
00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:22.080
But you can do your best to help other people do their best.


64
00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:34.080
I would tell somebody who's thinking about joining Linear that it's just an excellent job.


65
00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:39.540
And I think if you're ready for something that is sustainable,


66
00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:45.290
where you work with really great people who really care about what they do


67
00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:51.910
and do excellent work and are building something that people really love,


68
00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:56.760
and actually need, then it's a great place to work.
